  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "Express $\\frac{4x+23}{(5-2x)(x+3)}$ as a sum of partial fractions.\n\n| $\\frac{4x+23}{(5-2x)(x+3)}=\\,$ | {{field-1}} | $+$ | {{field-2}} |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $5-2x$ |  | $x+3$ |",
  "worked_solution": "Our task is to find $A$ and $B$ such that\n\n$$\n\\frac{4x+23}{(5-2x)(x+3)}=\\frac{A}{5-2x}+\\frac{B}{x+3}.\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides by $(5-2x)(x+3)$ gives\n\n$$\n4x+23=A(x+3)+B(5-2x).\n$$\n\nFor $x=-3$, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(-3)+23 & =A(-3+3)+B(5-2(-3)) \\\\ 11 & =11B \\\\ B & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x=\\frac{5}{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(\\frac{5}{2})+23 & =A(\\frac{5}{2}+3)+B(5-2(\\frac{5}{2})) \\\\ 33 & =A(\\frac{11}{2}) \\\\ A & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{4x+23}{(5-2x)(x+3)}=\\frac{6}{5-2x}+\\frac{1}{x+3}.\n$$",
